\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Standard number systems are $\mathbb N,\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$. Specialized notation and index ranges follow the cited source and the definitions below.


A communication protocol specifies messages and responses between parties with local observations. A goal can be modeled by a success predicate or utility on the interaction; a semantic interpretation assigns meanings to the communicated symbols.
\paragraph{Record scope and qualifications.}
The catalogue title is retained as metadata. Named certificates, contracts, bounds or frameworks in that title are not asserted as results of the original source extract.
Intelligence, understanding, meaning, goals, and successful interaction are not formalized in the supplied record. The text is a conceptual research direction.

Source-record limitation: Intelligence, understanding, goals, and meaningful communication are not given mathematical definitions or a determinate target.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Develop a mathematical theory of universal semantic communication: formalize intelligence, understanding, goals, and meaningful interaction when sender and receiver may have different semantics, and characterize when cooperative parties can communicate successfully from their observations and responses. \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2007/31}{[S2]}
\paragraph{Source status.}
The supplied catalogue labels this source record open; that metadata was checked against the supplied snapshot on 2026-10-11. The label is preserved for provenance and is not an independent verification of present open status or mathematical correctness. The catalogue literature assessment is dated 2026-08-21.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Develop a theory of communication between parties that interpret messages differently.

